description:
Trigger: a CalendarEvent/set update patch whose top-level keys are JSON Pointer paths into a per-user property (RFC 8620 §5.3 PatchObject paths with '/' separator), e.g.

  {
    'accountId': 'acc',
    'update': {
      'ev1': {
        'alerts/abc/trigger': {...},
        'alerts/abc/action': 'display'
      }
    }
  }

is misrouted to update_object (the shared-property path) instead of update_per_user_properties (the per-user path).

Location: event.rs:356-362.

  let is_per_user_only = patch_val.as_object()
      .map(|m| m.iter().all(|(k, _)|
              jmap_calendars_types::is_per_user_calendar_event_property(k)))
      .unwrap_or(false);

The predicate is_per_user_calendar_event_property does a direct string-equality check against the fixed list ['keywords', 'color', 'freeBusyStatus', 'useDefaultAlerts', 'alerts']. crate-jmap-calendars-types/src/backend.rs:281 already has a test that asserts is_per_user_calendar_event_property('alerts/abc') returns FALSE.

So 'alerts/abc/trigger' goes through update_object, which means:
1. The shared 'updated' timestamp is bumped (draft §5.4: 'changes that affect only per-user properties MUST NOT bump the shared updated timestamp').
2. Any iTIP scheduling that update_calendar_event triggers on this path (when sendSchedulingMessages: true) fires for what should have been a per-user-only mutation — §5.9.2.1 says per-user-only updates do NOT generate iTIP REQUEST.

Both are wire-level observable. Other clients will see a phantom update notification on the shared 'updated' field, and recipients may receive iTIP messages that were not warranted.

Fix: check the first path segment, not the whole key. Roughly:

  m.iter().all(|(k, _)| {
      let head = k.split_once('/').map_or(k.as_str(), |(h, _)| h);
      jmap_calendars_types::is_per_user_calendar_event_property(head)
  })

Note RFC 6901 JSON Pointer escaping: '~0' is '~' and '~1' is '/', so a key like 'keywords~1foo' is the literal property name 'keywords/foo' which is NOT a sub-path of 'keywords'. The current predicate also gets this case wrong (returns false). The replacement using split_once('/') treats it correctly as a top-level key 'keywords~1foo' which is neither 'keywords' nor any other per-user property — invalidProperties at the backend.

Test gap: the existing routing tests (event.rs:1556-1656) cover only top-level property keys; the JSON Pointer sub-path case has no coverage.

P0 because it can cause inappropriate iTIP scheduling messages to be sent in production once a backend overrides update_calendar_event with real iTIP delivery — that is an externally-observable, off-spec wire-level effect.
